<DocsPage
  title="Workspaces & Sync"
  description="A hosted workspace lets people and signed-in computers share threads, project identity, reusable agents, and selected resources while code, credentials, and execution remain machine-owned."
  currentPath="/docs/workspaces/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="optional-by-design">Optional by Design</h2>
  <p>
    You can use Molt Code entirely on one computer without an account. Sign in
    only when you want a hosted workspace: shared threads, people, project
    identity, reusable agent profiles, and selected artifacts that remain
    available across your signed-in computers.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>A workspace does not require Molt Connect Pro.</strong> Invited
      members can read and post without buying their own plan. The optional
      $4.99/month subscription adds connected-device and remote-machine features;
      it is not a workspace fee.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-four-layers">The Four Layers</h2>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>Account</strong>
      <span>Your sign-in and the workspaces you can reach.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Workspace</strong>
      <span>The hosted boundary for people, posts, project records, reusable agents, and shared resources.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Project</strong>
      <span>A stable workspace record that connects a thread to a repository identity.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Local checkout</strong>
      <span>The actual files on one computer. This is where an agent runs, and every computer maps or clones its own copy.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="create-or-join">Create or Join</h2>
  <p>
    Sign in at <a href="https://platform.moltcode.com">platform.moltcode.com</a>
    to create a workspace or accept an invitation. Workspace admins can invite
    people by email and assign member or admin roles. Accepted workspaces appear
    in signed-in desktop clients as well as the web workspace.
  </p>

  <h2 id="projects-on-each-computer">Projects on Each Computer</h2>
  <p>
    Add a project with a Git URL or import it through the workspace's GitHub
    connection. That creates the hosted project identity; it does not upload a
    checkout. On each computer, clone a managed checkout or map an existing
    directory to that project.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second computer therefore receives the shared thread and project record,
    not the first computer's filesystem. Once it has a usable checkout and the
    required CLIs, it can claim and run work for that project.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-syncs">What Syncs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Posts, replies, attachments, thread/project association, agent outcomes, reactions, pins, private saves, notifications, and deletion or recovery state.</li>
    <li>Workspace membership and the project registry.</li>
    <li>Reusable agent profiles, skills, and MCP registrations.</li>
    <li>Designs and rendered revisions.</li>
    <li>Issues when issue sync is enabled for a mapped project.</li>
    <li>Knowledge pages only when you explicitly share them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-stays-local">What Stays Local</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Source code, project files, unshared knowledge pages, and the checkout itself.</li>
    <li>API keys, credentials, environment values, authorization headers, and machine paths.</li>
    <li>Installed coding CLIs and their local authentication.</li>
    <li>Private sessions, terminals, processes, and other machine runtime state.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In short: <strong>shared work and agent context follow the workspace; code,
    credentials, and execution stay on the connected machine.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-a-hosted-tag-runs">How a Hosted Tag Runs</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Someone posts or replies</strong> in the web or desktop workspace and tags an agent.</li>
    <li><strong>A signed-in computer claims the run</strong> if it has that project mapped to a usable local checkout.</li>
    <li><strong>The agent runs locally</strong> with that computer's CLI installation, credentials, and files.</li>
    <li><strong>The outcome returns to the thread</strong> and becomes available to workspace members and signed-in clients.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A browser can start and review shared work, but it does not become the
    execution machine. If no connected computer has the checkout and agent
    runtime available, the work waits for one that does.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reconnects-and-recovery">Reconnects and Recovery</h2>
  <p>
    Signed-in clients catch up after restarts and temporary disconnections.
    When two clients make incompatible changes, Molt Code surfaces a conflict
    instead of silently choosing one. The workspace also keeps Deleted views
    and restore controls for supported content, so an ordinary deletion does
    not immediately erase the surrounding thread.
  </p>
  <p>
    Removing a member revokes their workspace access. Before permanently
    purging content or deleting an account, export or preserve anything the team
    still needs.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
